 eh@cunixb.cc.columbia.edu (Erik Hilsdale) writes:

> this isn't directly related to graphics, but it is related to getting
> the most out of whatever terminal/computer the NetHack is running on.

> how about making the number of terminal rows and columns settable from
> outside: i.e., either from nethack.cnf or from the command line? i
> haven't seen the code for some time (3.0 pl 7 maybe?), so i may be
> totally off here, but i seem to remember that there were #defines for
> the number of rows and columns avaliable to create the mazes.

> if these were made variables set upon startup, anyone working on an
> 80x43 or 80x50 (or whatever) screen would have that much more space
> per level to play with. in short, nethack would have a simple way to
> simulate the large mazes of moria without the programming and
> interface problems associated with scrolling the map.

I _really_ like this suggestion, and made one like it in perhaps 1988.

My home system (an Amiga 2000) supports (with, granted, a pretty horrid
font), up to 66 lines by about 160 characters. If the current four lines
of status/prompt lines were even increased to six, this would be (60/20)
times (160/80) or six times the play area.

To just do what is being done now, one would go from a maximum of ten
rooms to sixty, or make the rooms much larger.  If the latter choice
were made, I have an auxiliary suggstion.

I just got a chance to play "larn", and occasionally, it creates some
levels that are divided up by diagonal, rather than horizontal or
vertical lines, and with a nice assortment of archways, closed doors,
opened doors, and closed, trapped and magic trapped doors.

The structure of these levels is very pretty and interesting to explore,
and satisfys the old wish for a "cavelike" appearance to hack/nethack
dungeon levels.

If nethack were played on much larger screens, with still only ten
rooms, it would be really nice to include the larn code and give the
hack rectangular rooms the larn diagonal room structure _internally_,
making a much more interesting environment to explore.

Even on the current, lowest common denominator screens, some levels with
the larn room structure would be a nice change of pace.

There are problems with game balance if you make level 1 a lot bigger,
but I'd rather ignore them.  For me, the biggest fun of nethack is how
incredibly _complex_ the game is, and I think this would be an extension
in the right direction.

(I'd also like the game better if the levels were larger than the
screen, with a paging system that recentered the level about the
adventurer any time an approach within 2 spaces of the edge of the
screen following a hallway, were made, or centered on the room when a
new room is entered, but that is a separate set of changes from making
use of the current larger resolution on graphics screen monitors. What
it would do is let those of us of advancing years play the higher
complexity games with more readible text sizes.)

